# Accumulator Alias Wrong-Code Repair

schema_version: 1
directive: `self-declared P0 while executing INBOX-45`
target: `x86_64-linux-gnu`
validation_tier: 3
result: pass

The accepted baseline is `7c76c391`. Focused native proof for the in-flight
INBOX-45 comparison capability exposed pre-existing wrong-code in
`compiler_rt.floatunsihf.__floatunsihf`: input `1` returned raw half bits
`0xb800` (-0.5) instead of `0x3c00` (1.0). Disassembly showed the new i6
comparison branching correctly, but the final exponent used the later XOR
value where the IR required the still-live result of `trunc i32` to `i6`.

The root cause was shared scalar immediate-binary lowering. A no-op integer
truncation can create multiple value bindings for EAX. Before destructive
`shl`, `ashr`, `and`, `or`, `add`, `sub`, or `xor`, the lowering preserved
only one arbitrary live EAX binding. Any other live alias silently observed
the operation result. The repair applies the existing accumulator-liveness
discipline used by compare and named-binary lowering: preserve every live
accumulator binding before the destructive operation.

The permanent mmap regression combines `llvm.ctlz.i32`, a no-op `i32` to `i6`
truncation, an immediate XOR, and later uses of both aliased values. Against
the accepted baseline it fails deterministically (`expected 127007, found
126976`); with the repair, four exact cases pass. The full unit suite passes
967/967. Tier 2 passes 24/24 steps and 975/975 tests, including structural,
assembly, encoding, object, executable, and integrated-executable coverage.
The mandatory wrong-code Tier 3 passes all 17 subgates.

```text
regression before repair: expected 127007, found 126976
focused tests: 967/967
tier2: 24/24 steps; 975/975 tests
tier3: 17/17 subgates; local_fail=0
closure accounting: none; this is a standalone correctness repair
```

